1-The browser/user sends a request.
2-The reverse proxy (such as Nginx or Internet Information Services) receives it.
3-The reverse proxy forwards the request to Kestrel.
4-Kestrel runs the ASP.NET Core app and sends the response back through the reverse proxy.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🛡️
Mode 2 — Behind a Proxy
Nginx/IIS faces the internet, forwards to Kestrel internally.
🌍 → 🛎️ Proxy → ⚙️ Kestrel → 📦 App
⭐ Production recommended — we'll see why
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
// ↑ Kestrel is already configured here — no extra setup needed
 
var app = builder.Build();
app.MapGet("/", () => "Hello from Kestrel!");
app.Run();
// New project templates auto-pick random ports per project:
//   HTTP  → some port in 5000-5300
//   HTTPS → some port in 7000-7300
// Stored in Properties/launchSettings.json — you can change them anytime.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Kestrel's defaults work for development but production deployments often need custom ports, certificate paths, 
and request size limits. All of this is configurable in code or via appsettings.json.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
  "Kestrel": {
    "Endpoints": {
      "Http": {
        "Url": "http://0.0.0.0:5000"
      },
      "Https": {
        "Url": "https://0.0.0.0:5001",
        "Certificate": {
          "Path":     "certs/myapp.pfx",
          "Password": "s3cr3t"
        }
      }
    },
    "Limits": {
      "MaxRequestBodySize":         10485760,  // 10 MB
      "MaxConcurrentConnections":    100,
      "KeepAliveTimeout":           "00:02:00"
    }
  }
}
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A reverse proxy sits between the internet and Kestrel. Clients talk to the proxy — the proxy forwards requests to Kestrel, 
gets the response, and sends it back. To the outside world, Kestrel is invisible. This unlocks capabilities Kestrel 
deliberately doesn't implement.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
reverse proxy add the next -> 
----------------------------
🔒SSL Termination
Handles HTTPS cert renewal; Kestrel sees plain HTTP internally

⚖️Load Balancing
Distribute traffic across multiple Kestrel instances

🛡️Security Hardening
Rate limiting, IP filtering, Web Application Firewall rules at the edge

📁Static Files
Nginx serves CSS/JS/images far faster than Kestrel

🔁Request Buffering
Absorbs slow clients so Kestrel threads aren't blocked
